<code>assert (e == null) => (this.peekLast() == null); assert (e != null) => (this.peekLast().equals(e));</code>

However, since we need to combine these conditions into a single assert statement, we can use the conditional operator to achieve this:

<code>assert (e == null) == (this.peekLast() == null);</code>

This single assert statement captures both conditions: if `e` is null, then the last element of the list should be null, and if `e` is not null, then the last element of the list should be equal to `e`.
--------------------
<code>assert (e == null) => (this.peekLast() == null) && (e != null) => (this.peekLast() == e);</code>

This can be simplified to:

<code>assert this.peekLast() == e;</code>

Since if `e` is `null`, `this.peekLast()` will also be `null`, and if `e` is not `null`, `this.peekLast()` will be equal to `e`. The simplified version captures both conditions succinctly.
